Return ssize_t from libs3's neon body provider.

neon declares the provider as ssize_t (*)(void *, char *, size_t); libs3's
neon_read_func returns int. On 32-bit Windows, the only target upstream
builds, both are 32 bits wide and the call is fine. On x86-64 the caller
reads all of RAX while the callee sets only EAX: the -1 that aborts an
upload would arrive as 4294967295, a byte count. As C++, which libs3 is
compiled as, the mismatch does not even convert. The function's returns
are all small values, so widening the type is the whole fix; it is right on
32-bit too. Line counts are unchanged.

--- a/libs/libs3/src/request.c	2026-10-11 13:48:18.130543088 -0300
+++ b/libs/libs3/src/request.c	2026-10-11 13:48:18.135647425 -0300
@@ -202,7 +202,7 @@
 
 
 // WINSCP (neon)
-static int neon_read_func(void * userdata, char * buf, size_t len)
+static ssize_t neon_read_func(void * userdata, char * buf, size_t len)
 {
     Request *request = (Request *) userdata;
 
